const CARDS_OPHTHO_GLAUCOMA = [
// ===== AQUEOUS HUMOR DYNAMICS =====
{ q: "Where is aqueous humor produced and at what rate?", a: "<b>Ciliary body epithelium</b> (non-pigmented layer) produces aqueous humor at approximately <b>2.5 μL/min</b> (~2.0–2.5 μL/min)." },
{ q: "What are the two mechanisms of aqueous humor production?", a: "1. <b>Active secretion</b> (major) — Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase & carbonic anhydrase–dependent<br>2. <b>Ultrafiltration & diffusion</b> (minor) — pressure-dependent" },
{ q: "Describe the conventional outflow pathway of aqueous humor.", a: "<b>Posterior chamber</b> → through <b>pupil</b> → <b>anterior chamber</b> → <b>trabecular meshwork</b> → <b>Schlemm canal</b> → collector channels → <b>episcleral veins</b><br>Accounts for <b>80–90%</b> of total outflow." },
{ q: "What is the uveoscleral (unconventional) outflow pathway?", a: "Aqueous flows through the <b>ciliary muscle interstitial spaces</b> → <b>suprachoroidal space</b> → absorbed by choroidal vasculature or through sclera.<br>Accounts for <b>10–20%</b> of outflow. <b>Prostaglandin analogs</b> enhance this pathway." },
{ q: "What is the composition of aqueous humor?", a: "Similar to plasma but with <b>lower protein</b> (~1/500th of plasma), <b>lower glucose</b>, <b>higher ascorbate</b>, and <b>higher lactate</b>. Maintains <b>transparency</b> of the lens and cornea and provides <b>nutrients</b> to avascular structures." },
{ q: "What enzyme in the ciliary epithelium is targeted by carbonic anhydrase inhibitors?", a: "<b>Carbonic anhydrase</b> — catalyzes: CO₂ + H₂O → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺<br>Inhibition reduces <b>bicarbonate and sodium transport</b> into the posterior chamber, thereby <b>decreasing aqueous humor production</b>." },

// ===== INTRAOCULAR PRESSURE =====
{ q: "What is the normal range of intraocular pressure (IOP)?", a: "<b>10–21 mmHg</b><br>Mean ~15.5 mmHg. IOP >21 mmHg is considered statistically elevated but does <b>not</b> define glaucoma by itself." },
{ q: "When is IOP typically highest during the day?", a: "IOP is highest in the <b>early morning</b> (often around 6–8 AM) due to <b>diurnal variation</b>.<br>Variation of <b>2–6 mmHg</b> is normal; >10 mmHg fluctuation is suspicious for glaucoma." },
{ q: "What is the gold standard method for measuring IOP?", a: "<b>Goldmann applanation tonometry (GAT)</b><br>Based on the <b>Imbert-Fick principle</b>: pressure inside a sphere = force needed to flatten (applanate) a known area (3.06 mm diameter)." },
{ q: "How does central corneal thickness (CCT) affect IOP measurement?", a: "<b>Thin cornea</b> (<555 μm) → <b>underestimates</b> true IOP<br><b>Thick cornea</b> (>588 μm) → <b>overestimates</b> true IOP<br>Every 10 μm change ≈ 0.5 mmHg correction. Thin CCT is an <b>independent risk factor</b> for POAG." },
{ q: "Name alternative IOP measurement methods besides Goldmann tonometry.", a: "1. <b>Tono-Pen</b> — portable, handheld<br>2. <b>Non-contact tonometry</b> (air puff) — screening tool<br>3. <b>iCare</b> (rebound tonometry) — no anesthesia needed, good for children<br>4. <b>Pneumatonometry</b><br>5. <b>Perkins tonometer</b> — portable Goldmann" },

// ===== OPTIC NERVE ANATOMY =====
{ q: "What is a normal cup-to-disc (C/D) ratio?", a: "Normal C/D ratio is <b><0.5</b><br>A C/D ratio ≥0.5, <b>asymmetry >0.2</b> between eyes, or <b>progressive enlargement</b> of the cup are concerning for glaucoma." },
{ q: "What is the ISNT rule for the neuroretinal rim?", a: "The normal neuroretinal rim is thickest to thinnest: <b>I</b>nferior (thickest) > <b>S</b>uperior > <b>N</b>asal > <b>T</b>emporal (thinnest).<br>Violation of ISNT rule suggests <b>glaucomatous damage</b>." },
{ q: "What is the lamina cribrosa and its role in glaucoma?", a: "The <b>lamina cribrosa</b> is a sieve-like structure of connective tissue through which <b>retinal ganglion cell axons</b> pass as they exit the eye.<br>In glaucoma, elevated IOP causes <b>posterior bowing</b> of the lamina, leading to <b>axonal compression and ischemia</b>." },
{ q: "What is the retinal nerve fiber layer (RNFL) and how is it assessed?", a: "The <b>RNFL</b> consists of <b>retinal ganglion cell axons</b> coursing toward the optic disc.<br>Assessed by <b>OCT</b> (optical coherence tomography) — thinning of RNFL is an <b>early sign</b> of glaucomatous damage, often preceding visual field loss." },
{ q: "What structural changes occur at the optic disc in glaucoma?", a: "1. <b>Increased cupping</b> (↑ C/D ratio)<br>2. <b>Neuroretinal rim thinning/notching</b> (especially inferotemporal)<br>3. <b>Disc hemorrhage</b> (Drance hemorrhage)<br>4. <b>RNFL defects</b><br>5. <b>Bayoneting</b> of blood vessels<br>6. <b>Laminar dot sign</b><br>7. <b>Peripapillary atrophy</b> (beta zone)" },

// ===== POAG =====
{ q: "What is the most common type of glaucoma worldwide?", a: "<b>Primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG)</b><br>Accounts for ~70% of all glaucoma cases in Western countries. It is <b>chronic, bilateral, and painless</b>." },
{ q: "What are the key clinical features of POAG?", a: "1. <b>Open angle</b> on gonioscopy (grade III–IV)<br>2. <b>Elevated IOP</b> (usually >21 mmHg, but not always)<br>3. <b>Optic disc cupping</b> (↑ C/D ratio)<br>4. <b>Progressive visual field loss</b> (peripheral first)<br>5. <b>Painless</b> and <b>insidious onset</b><br>6. Central vision preserved until <b>late disease</b>" },
{ q: "What are the major risk factors for POAG?", a: "1. <b>Age >40</b> (prevalence increases with age)<br>2. <b>African American race</b> (4–5× higher risk)<br>3. <b>Family history</b> (first-degree relative)<br>4. <b>Elevated IOP</b><br>5. <b>Myopia</b><br>6. <b>Thin CCT</b> (<555 μm)<br>7. <b>Diabetes mellitus</b><br>8. <b>Disc hemorrhage</b>" },
{ q: "What is normal-tension glaucoma (NTG)?", a: "A subtype of POAG where <b>IOP is consistently <21 mmHg</b> but there is progressive <b>optic nerve damage</b> and <b>visual field loss</b>.<br>Associated with: <b>vasospasm, Raynaud phenomenon, migraine, nocturnal hypotension, sleep apnea</b>.<br>Treatment: <b>IOP lowering still beneficial</b> (CNTGS trial)." },
{ q: "Why is POAG often diagnosed late?", a: "Because it is <b>painless</b> and <b>peripheral vision</b> is lost first while <b>central vision is preserved until late</b>. Patients may not notice symptoms until <b>significant irreversible damage</b> has occurred. Hence routine screening is important." },

// ===== VISUAL FIELD DEFECTS =====
{ q: "What is the characteristic pattern of visual field loss in glaucoma?", a: "Glaucoma typically causes <b>peripheral (arcuate) field loss</b> first, progressing centrally:<br>1. <b>Paracentral scotoma</b> (earliest)<br>2. <b>Arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma</b><br>3. <b>Nasal step</b> (Roenne)<br>4. <b>Ring scotoma</b><br>5. <b>Generalized constriction</b> → tunnel vision<br>Central island and temporal island are <b>last to go</b>." },
{ q: "What is an arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma?", a: "An <b>arc-shaped visual field defect</b> following the distribution of the <b>arcuate nerve fiber bundle</b>, arching from the <b>blind spot</b> to the nasal horizontal meridian.<br>It is a <b>hallmark</b> of glaucomatous visual field loss." },
{ q: "What is a nasal step (Roenne nasal step)?", a: "A <b>visual field defect</b> at the <b>nasal horizontal meridian</b> where the upper and lower arcuate defects do not meet symmetrically, creating a <b>step</b>.<br>It is <b>highly specific</b> for glaucoma." },
{ q: "What visual field test is standard for glaucoma monitoring?", a: "<b>Humphrey visual field (HVF)</b> using the <b>24-2</b> or <b>30-2</b> SITA strategy.<br>Key parameters:<br>- <b>Mean deviation (MD)</b>: overall field sensitivity loss<br>- <b>Pattern standard deviation (PSD)</b>: localized field defects<br>- <b>Glaucoma Hemifield Test (GHT)</b>: compares superior and inferior hemifields" },
{ q: "What do mean deviation (MD) and pattern standard deviation (PSD) indicate on HVF?", a: "<b>MD (Mean Deviation)</b>: overall reduction in sensitivity across the entire visual field; more negative = worse<br><b>PSD (Pattern Standard Deviation)</b>: measures <b>localized</b> (focal) defects; elevated PSD with normal MD suggests <b>early glaucoma</b>" },

// ===== DIAGNOSTIC WORKUP =====
{ q: "What is gonioscopy and what does it assess?", a: "<b>Gonioscopy</b> uses a contact lens with mirrors to visualize the <b>iridocorneal angle</b> (drainage angle).<br>Determines if the angle is <b>open or closed</b> and identifies pathology (neovascularization, synechiae, recession, pigment)." },
{ q: "Describe the Shaffer grading system for gonioscopy.", a: "<b>Grade IV</b> (35–45°): wide open — all structures visible<br><b>Grade III</b> (25–35°): open — scleral spur visible<br><b>Grade II</b> (20°): moderately narrow — trabecular meshwork visible<br><b>Grade I</b> (10°): very narrow — only Schwalbe line visible<br><b>Grade 0</b> (0°): closed — no structures visible, <b>closure risk</b>" },
{ q: "What structures are seen on gonioscopy from anterior to posterior?", a: "Mnemonic: <b>'She Took Sam's Car'</b><br>1. <b>S</b>chwalbe line<br>2. <b>T</b>rabecular meshwork (pigmented & non-pigmented)<br>3. <b>S</b>cleral spur<br>4. <b>C</b>iliary body band" },
{ q: "What is the role of OCT in glaucoma evaluation?", a: "<b>OCT (Optical Coherence Tomography)</b> measures <b>RNFL thickness</b>, <b>ganglion cell complex (GCC)</b>, and <b>optic disc parameters</b>.<br>Detects <b>structural damage before functional loss</b> on visual fields. Used for <b>diagnosis and progression monitoring</b>." },
{ q: "What is pachymetry and why is it important in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Pachymetry</b> measures <b>central corneal thickness (CCT)</b>.<br>Normal CCT ≈ <b>540–560 μm</b>.<br>Thin CCT (<555 μm) is an independent risk factor for POAG and <b>underestimates IOP</b> on GAT. Critical for risk stratification." },

// ===== MEDICAL MANAGEMENT OF POAG =====
{ q: "What is the target IOP in treating POAG?", a: "Typically a <b>20–30% reduction from baseline IOP</b>.<br>Target is individualized based on: <b>severity of damage, rate of progression, life expectancy, baseline IOP</b>.<br>Lower targets (e.g., <12 mmHg) for advanced disease or NTG." },
{ q: "What is the first-line medical treatment for POAG?", a: "<b>Prostaglandin analogs (PGAs)</b>: latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost, tafluprost<br>Mechanism: <b>increase uveoscleral outflow</b><br>Dosing: <b>once daily</b> (evening)<br>Most effective single agents, reducing IOP by <b>25–33%</b>." },
{ q: "What are the side effects of prostaglandin analogs?", a: "1. <b>Iris darkening</b> (↑ melanin, irreversible)<br>2. <b>Eyelash growth</b> (hypertrichosis) — used cosmetically as bimatoprost (Latisse)<br>3. <b>Periorbital fat atrophy</b> (prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy, PAP)<br>4. <b>Conjunctival hyperemia</b><br>5. <b>Cystoid macular edema</b> (caution in uveitis/pseudophakia)<br>6. <b>Darkening of periocular skin</b>" },
{ q: "How do beta-blockers work in glaucoma and what are key contraindications?", a: "<b>Timolol (non-selective β-blocker)</b> — <b>decreases aqueous production</b> by blocking β2 receptors on ciliary epithelium.<br>IOP reduction: <b>20–25%</b>. Given BID.<br><b>Contraindications</b>: asthma/COPD, bradycardia, heart block, decompensated CHF. Can cause <b>bronchospasm, bradycardia, hypotension, depression</b>." },
{ q: "What is the mechanism and key side effect concern with brimonidine?", a: "<b>Brimonidine</b> (alpha-2 agonist):<br>Mechanism: <b>decreases aqueous production</b> + <b>increases uveoscleral outflow</b><br>IOP reduction: ~20%<br><b>Avoid in children <2 years old</b> — risk of <b>CNS depression</b> (apnea, lethargy, bradycardia, hypotension).<br>Other SE: allergic conjunctivitis, dry mouth, fatigue." },
{ q: "Compare topical vs oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (CAIs).", a: "<b>Topical CAIs</b>: dorzolamide, brinzolamide — fewer systemic SE, BID-TID dosing<br><b>Oral CAIs</b>: acetazolamide, methazolamide — more potent but significant SE:<br>- <b>Metabolic acidosis</b> (non-anion gap)<br>- <b>Paresthesias</b> (tingling fingers/lips)<br>- <b>Hypokalemia</b><br>- <b>Kidney stones</b><br>- <b>Aplastic anemia</b> (rare, idiosyncratic)<br>- <b>Metallic taste</b><br><b>Avoid in sulfa allergy</b>" },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of Rho kinase inhibitors in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Netarsudil</b> (Rhopressa) — <b>Rho kinase (ROCK) inhibitor</b><br>Mechanism: <b>increases trabecular meshwork outflow</b> by relaxing TM cells + decreases episcleral venous pressure + decreases aqueous production.<br>Dosed <b>once daily</b>. SE: conjunctival hyperemia, corneal verticillata." },
{ q: "How do miotics (pilocarpine) work and what are their side effects?", a: "<b>Pilocarpine</b> — <b>direct-acting muscarinic agonist (miotic)</b><br>Mechanism: contracts <b>ciliary muscle</b> → pulls open trabecular meshwork → <b>increases trabecular outflow</b><br>SE: <b>miosis</b>, brow ache, accommodative myopia, dim vision, <b>increased risk of retinal detachment</b> (due to vitreal traction)." },
{ q: "Which glaucoma medication class reduces IOP by increasing trabecular outflow?", a: "1. <b>Miotics (pilocarpine)</b> — ciliary muscle contraction opens TM<br>2. <b>Rho kinase inhibitors (netarsudil)</b> — relax TM cells<br>(Prostaglandin analogs increase <b>uveoscleral</b> outflow, not trabecular)" },
{ q: "Which glaucoma medications decrease aqueous humor production?", a: "1. <b>Beta-blockers</b> (timolol, betaxolol)<br>2. <b>Alpha-2 agonists</b> (brimonidine, apraclonidine)<br>3. <b>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</b> (dorzolamide, acetazolamide)<br>All three classes act on the <b>ciliary body epithelium</b> to reduce secretion." },
{ q: "What is the role of betaxolol in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Betaxolol</b> is a <b>selective β1-blocker</b> (cardioselective).<br>Less effective than timolol (non-selective) for IOP reduction but <b>safer in patients with pulmonary disease</b> (asthma/COPD) because it has minimal β2 blockade. Still use with caution." },
{ q: "What fixed-combination glaucoma drops are commonly used?", a: "1. <b>Cosopt</b> = dorzolamide + timolol<br>2. <b>Combigan</b> = brimonidine + timolol<br>3. <b>Simbrinza</b> = brimonidine + brinzolamide<br>4. <b>Rocklatan</b> = netarsudil + latanoprost<br>Fixed combinations improve <b>compliance</b> and reduce <b>preservative exposure</b>." },

// ===== LASER TREATMENT =====
{ q: "What is selective laser trabeculoplasty (SLT)?", a: "<b>SLT</b> uses a <b>frequency-doubled Nd:YAG laser (532 nm)</b> to selectively target <b>pigmented trabecular meshwork cells</b>.<br>Stimulates <b>macrophage recruitment</b> → remodels TM → improves outflow.<br>IOP reduction: <b>20–30%</b>. <b>Repeatable</b> (minimal tissue destruction). Can be used as <b>first-line therapy</b> (LiGHT trial)." },
{ q: "How does SLT differ from ALT?", a: "<b>SLT</b>: selective, minimal thermal damage, <b>repeatable</b>, targets pigmented cells only<br><b>ALT (Argon Laser Trabeculoplasty)</b>: causes <b>coagulative damage</b> to TM, <b>not easily repeatable</b>, risk of PAS formation<br>SLT has largely <b>replaced ALT</b> in clinical practice." },
{ q: "What is laser peripheral iridotomy (LPI) and when is it used?", a: "<b>LPI</b> creates a small <b>full-thickness hole in the peripheral iris</b> using Nd:YAG laser.<br>Indication: <b>angle closure</b> (acute or chronic) due to <b>pupillary block</b>.<br>Equalizes pressure between posterior and anterior chambers → relieves pupillary block → opens the angle.<br>Perform in <b>both eyes</b> (fellow eye prophylaxis)." },

// ===== SURGICAL MANAGEMENT =====
{ q: "What is a trabeculectomy?", a: "A <b>filtering surgery</b> that creates a <b>fistula</b> from the anterior chamber to the <b>subconjunctival space</b>.<br>Aqueous drains under the conjunctiva forming a <b>bleb</b>.<br><b>Antimetabolites</b> (5-FU, mitomycin C) applied to prevent <b>fibrosis/scarring</b> of the bleb." },
{ q: "What are the complications of trabeculectomy?", a: "Early: <b>hypotony, shallow AC, choroidal effusion, hyphema, bleb leak</b> (Seidel test positive)<br>Late: <b>bleb infection (blebitis), endophthalmitis, bleb encapsulation/failure, cataract formation</b><br>Antimetabolites increase risk of <b>thin avascular blebs</b> and late-onset bleb leaks." },
{ q: "What are glaucoma tube shunt (drainage) devices?", a: "Tubes that shunt aqueous from the <b>AC to a plate</b> in the subconjunctival/sub-Tenon space.<br><b>Ahmed valve</b>: has a flow restrictor (valved) — less early hypotony<br><b>Baerveldt implant</b>: non-valved, larger plate — may achieve lower IOP<br>Used for <b>refractory or complicated glaucoma</b> (failed trabeculectomy, neovascular glaucoma)." },
{ q: "What is MIGS (minimally invasive glaucoma surgery)?", a: "<b>MIGS</b> = Minimally Invasive Glaucoma Surgery<br>Examples: <b>iStent</b> (TM bypass to Schlemm canal), <b>Kahook dual blade</b> (goniotomy), <b>XEN gel stent</b> (subconjunctival drainage), <b>Hydrus microstent</b>, <b>GATT</b><br>Advantages: <b>less invasive, faster recovery, fewer complications</b> than trabeculectomy. Best for <b>mild-moderate glaucoma</b>, often combined with cataract surgery." },
{ q: "What are cyclodestructive procedures and when are they used?", a: "<b>Cyclophotocoagulation (CPC)</b> — destroys <b>ciliary body epithelium</b> to <b>reduce aqueous production</b>.<br>Types: <b>transscleral CPC</b>, <b>endoscopic CPC (ECP)</b>, <b>micropulse CPC</b><br>Reserved as <b>last resort</b> for refractory glaucoma or eyes with <b>poor visual potential</b>. Risk of <b>phthisis bulbi</b>." },

// ===== PRIMARY ANGLE-CLOSURE GLAUCOMA =====
{ q: "What is the pathophysiology of primary angle-closure glaucoma (PACG)?", a: "<b>Pupillary block</b> is the most common mechanism: the iris bows forward against the lens → blocks aqueous flow through the pupil → iris bombé → peripheral iris occludes the <b>trabecular meshwork</b> → acute IOP elevation." },
{ q: "What anatomic factors predispose to angle closure?", a: "1. <b>Shallow anterior chamber</b><br>2. <b>Short axial length</b> (hyperopia)<br>3. <b>Thick lens</b> (increases with age)<br>4. <b>Anteriorly positioned lens</b><br>5. <b>Small corneal diameter</b><br>Demographics: <b>Asian descent, female, age >50</b>" },
{ q: "What precipitates an acute angle-closure attack?", a: "1. <b>Dim illumination</b> (mid-dilated pupil = maximum pupillary block)<br>2. <b>Mydriatic drops</b> (phenylephrine, tropicamide, atropine)<br>3. <b>Emotional stress/excitement</b><br>4. <b>Prone positioning</b><br>5. <b>Anticholinergic/sympathomimetic medications</b><br>The pupil reaches <b>mid-dilation</b>, maximizing iris-lens contact." },
{ q: "What are the symptoms of acute angle-closure glaucoma?", a: "1. <b>Severe eye pain</b> (unilateral)<br>2. <b>Headache</b> (often frontal)<br>3. <b>Nausea and vomiting</b> (vagal response — can mimic acute abdomen)<br>4. <b>Halos around lights</b> (corneal edema)<br>5. <b>Decreased/blurred vision</b><br>6. <b>Red eye</b>" },
{ q: "What are the signs of acute angle-closure glaucoma on examination?", a: "1. <b>Mid-dilated, fixed (non-reactive) pupil</b><br>2. <b>Corneal edema</b> (hazy/steamy cornea)<br>3. <b>Shallow anterior chamber</b><br>4. <b>IOP 40–80 mmHg</b><br>5. <b>Ciliary injection</b> (circumcorneal flush)<br>6. <b>Closed angle on gonioscopy</b><br>7. <b>Glaukomflecken</b> (anterior subcapsular lens opacities from prior attacks)" },
{ q: "What is the emergency medical treatment for acute angle closure?", a: "1. <b>Timolol 0.5%</b> — ↓ aqueous production<br>2. <b>Brimonidine 0.2%</b> — ↓ aqueous production<br>3. <b>Pilocarpine 2%</b> — constricts pupil, pulls iris from angle (give <b>after IOP begins to drop</b>; iris sphincter ischemic at very high IOP)<br>4. <b>Topical steroid</b> (prednisolone) — ↓ inflammation<br>5. <b>Oral/IV acetazolamide 500 mg</b><br>6. <b>IV mannitol</b> (osmotic) if refractory" },
{ q: "What is the definitive treatment for acute angle closure?", a: "<b>Laser peripheral iridotomy (LPI)</b> — performed in <b>BOTH eyes</b> (affected + fellow eye prophylaxis).<br>Creates a bypass channel through the iris, relieving <b>pupillary block</b>.<br>The fellow eye has a <b>40–80% risk</b> of an acute attack if untreated." },
{ q: "Why is pilocarpine initially ineffective in very high IOP during acute angle closure?", a: "At IOP >40–50 mmHg, the <b>iris sphincter muscle is ischemic</b> and paralyzed, making pilocarpine <b>ineffective</b>.<br>IOP must first be lowered with other agents (timolol, brimonidine, acetazolamide, mannitol) before pilocarpine can <b>constrict the pupil</b>." },
{ q: "What is plateau iris syndrome?", a: "<b>Plateau iris</b>: anteriorly positioned <b>ciliary processes</b> push the peripheral iris forward, causing angle closure <b>despite a patent iridotomy</b>.<br>Diagnosis: <b>ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM)</b> or anterior segment OCT.<br>Treatment: <b>argon laser peripheral iridoplasty (ALPI)</b> — contracts peripheral iris stroma away from angle." },
{ q: "What is iris bombé?", a: "<b>Iris bombé</b> = forward bowing of the entire iris due to <b>360° posterior synechiae</b> (iris adhered to lens) blocking aqueous flow from posterior to anterior chamber.<br>Causes <b>secondary angle closure</b>.<br>Treatment: <b>LPI</b> to create an alternate aqueous pathway + treat underlying cause (usually <b>uveitis</b>)." },
{ q: "What are glaukomflecken?", a: "<b>Glaukomflecken</b> = small, white <b>anterior subcapsular lens opacities</b> seen after a previous episode of <b>acute angle-closure glaucoma</b>.<br>Caused by <b>ischemic necrosis of the lens epithelium</b> during the acute IOP spike. They are a <b>hallmark sign</b> of prior acute angle closure." },

// ===== SECONDARY GLAUCOMAS =====
{ q: "What is neovascular glaucoma (NVG) and what causes it?", a: "<b>NVG</b> = secondary glaucoma caused by <b>neovascularization of the iris (rubeosis iridis)</b> and angle.<br>Causes: <b>proliferative diabetic retinopathy, CRVO</b> (100-day glaucoma), <b>ocular ischemic syndrome, CRAO</b><br>Mechanism: retinal ischemia → <b>VEGF</b> release → new vessels obstruct TM → <b>PAS and angle closure</b>." },
{ q: "Why is CRVO-associated NVG called '100-day glaucoma'?", a: "Neovascular glaucoma typically develops approximately <b>3 months (100 days)</b> after an <b>ischemic CRVO</b>.<br>Ischemic retina releases <b>VEGF</b> → rubeosis iridis → fibrovascular membrane covers angle → <b>synechial angle closure</b>." },
{ q: "How is neovascular glaucoma treated?", a: "1. <b>Anti-VEGF injection</b> (bevacizumab) — causes <b>rapid regression</b> of new vessels<br>2. <b>Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)</b> — treats underlying ischemia (definitive for retinal cause)<br>3. <b>IOP-lowering medications</b><br>4. <b>Glaucoma surgery</b> (tube shunt preferred over trabeculectomy — high failure rate with trab)<br>5. <b>Cyclophotocoagulation</b> if poor visual prognosis" },
{ q: "What is pseudoexfoliation (PXF) glaucoma?", a: "<b>PXF glaucoma</b> = secondary open-angle glaucoma associated with deposition of <b>white, dandruff-like fibrillar material</b> on the <b>anterior lens capsule, pupillary margin, and trabecular meshwork</b>.<br>Associated with <b>zonular weakness</b> (risk during cataract surgery), higher IOP, and <b>poorer response to medical therapy</b>.<br>Most common identifiable cause of OAG worldwide." },
{ q: "What is the classic pattern of pseudoexfoliation material on the lens?", a: "A <b>three-ring (bull's-eye) pattern</b> on the anterior lens capsule:<br>1. <b>Central disc</b> of exfoliative material<br>2. <b>Clear intermediate zone</b> (scraped clean by pupillary movement)<br>3. <b>Peripheral granular zone</b><br>Best seen with <b>dilated exam</b> at the slit lamp." },
{ q: "What is pigmentary glaucoma?", a: "<b>Pigmentary glaucoma</b> = secondary OAG due to <b>pigment liberation</b> from the posterior iris surface.<br>Classic findings:<br>- <b>Krukenberg spindle</b> (vertical pigment deposition on corneal endothelium)<br>- <b>Concave (posteriorly bowed) iris</b> — rubs against lens zonules<br>- <b>Mid-peripheral iris transillumination defects</b><br>- Heavy TM pigmentation<br>Typical patient: <b>young, myopic male</b>." },
{ q: "Why can exercise trigger IOP spikes in pigmentary glaucoma?", a: "Exercise causes <b>pupillary dilation and accommodation changes</b> → increased <b>iridozonular contact</b> → <b>pigment shower</b> from posterior iris surface → acute TM obstruction → <b>IOP spike</b>.<br>Patients may report <b>halos and blurred vision</b> after vigorous exercise." },
{ q: "What is steroid-induced glaucoma?", a: "<b>Steroid-induced glaucoma</b> = secondary <b>open-angle glaucoma</b> caused by corticosteroid use.<br>Route risk: <b>topical > periocular > systemic > inhaled</b><br>Mechanism: <b>glycosaminoglycan accumulation</b> and <b>myocilin deposition</b> in TM → decreased outflow<br>Usually <b>reversible</b> upon discontinuation. <b>Steroid responders</b> (~30% of population) are at risk." },
{ q: "What lens-related (phacogenic) glaucomas exist?", a: "1. <b>Phacomorphic</b>: <b>swollen/intumescent lens</b> → pushes iris forward → angle closure<br>2. <b>Phacolytic</b>: <b>high-molecular-weight lens proteins</b> leak through intact capsule of a mature cataract → macrophage-laden proteins clog TM → open-angle glaucoma<br>3. <b>Lens particle</b>: retained lens material after surgery/trauma clogs TM<br>4. <b>Phacoantigenic (phacoanaphylactic)</b>: granulomatous inflammation from lens protein exposure" },
{ q: "How do you differentiate phacomorphic from phacolytic glaucoma?", a: "<b>Phacomorphic</b>: <b>closed angle</b>, intumescent (swollen) cataract, shallow AC<br>Treatment: IOP control → <b>cataract extraction</b> ± LPI<br><br><b>Phacolytic</b>: <b>open angle</b>, mature/hypermature cataract, <b>heavy flare</b> without keratic precipitates, macrophages in AC<br>Treatment: IOP control → <b>cataract extraction</b>" },
{ q: "How does trauma cause glaucoma?", a: "1. <b>Angle recession</b> — tear of ciliary body face; presents months-years later as OAG; bilateral gonioscopy important<br>2. <b>Hyphema</b> — blood in AC clogs TM (especially <b>sickle cell</b> patients — avoid CAIs and epinephrine)<br>3. <b>Lens dislocation</b> → pupillary block<br>4. <b>Ghost cell glaucoma</b> — denatured RBCs from old vitreous hemorrhage" },
{ q: "What is the significance of angle recession on gonioscopy?", a: "<b>Angle recession</b> = widened <b>ciliary body band</b> on gonioscopy due to a <b>tear between the longitudinal and circular muscles</b> of the ciliary body.<br><b>Unilateral</b> widening of the CB band compared to the fellow eye is diagnostic.<br>≥180° of recession → <b>higher risk</b> of late-onset OAG. Requires <b>lifelong monitoring</b>." },
{ q: "What is inflammatory glaucoma?", a: "Glaucoma secondary to <b>uveitis</b>. Mechanisms:<br>1. <b>Trabeculitis</b> — inflammation of TM → decreased outflow (open angle)<br>2. <b>Peripheral anterior synechiae (PAS)</b> → angle closure<br>3. <b>Posterior synechiae → iris bombé</b> → angle closure<br>4. <b>Steroid-induced</b> (from treatment of uveitis)<br>Treatment: control inflammation + IOP-lowering drops ± surgery." },

// ===== CONGENITAL/DEVELOPMENTAL GLAUCOMA =====
{ q: "What is primary congenital glaucoma and when does it present?", a: "<b>Primary congenital glaucoma</b> = developmental anomaly of the <b>trabecular meshwork (trabeculodysgenesis)</b>.<br>Presents in the <b>first year of life</b> (65% by 6 months).<br>More common in <b>males</b>. Often <b>bilateral</b> (75%).<br>Inheritance: usually <b>sporadic</b>, some autosomal recessive (CYP1B1 gene)." },
{ q: "What is buphthalmos?", a: "<b>Buphthalmos</b> ('ox eye') = <b>enlarged globe</b> in congenital glaucoma.<br>Occurs because the infant sclera is <b>distensible</b> — elevated IOP causes stretching of the entire globe.<br>Results in <b>increased corneal diameter</b> (>12 mm at birth), <b>increased axial length</b>, and <b>myopia</b>." },
{ q: "What are Haab striae?", a: "<b>Haab striae</b> = <b>horizontal breaks (tears) in Descemet membrane</b> caused by stretching of the cornea due to elevated IOP in congenital glaucoma.<br>They are <b>horizontal or curvilinear</b> (vs. vertical in forceps injury).<br>Lead to <b>corneal edema</b> and can cause <b>amblyopia</b>." },
{ q: "What is the classic triad of congenital glaucoma?", a: "1. <b>Epiphora</b> (tearing)<br>2. <b>Photophobia</b> (light sensitivity)<br>3. <b>Blepharospasm</b> (eyelid squeezing)<br>All due to <b>corneal edema</b> from elevated IOP. Parents often notice the child is <b>light-averse</b> and has <b>large, cloudy eyes</b>." },
{ q: "What is the primary treatment for congenital glaucoma?", a: "Treatment is <b>surgical</b> (medical therapy alone is insufficient):<br>1. <b>Goniotomy</b> — incision into TM under direct gonioscopic view (requires clear cornea)<br>2. <b>Trabeculotomy</b> — ab externo approach, doesn't require clear cornea<br>Success rate: <b>80–90%</b> with one or two procedures. Medical therapy is <b>adjunctive only</b>." },
{ q: "What systemic conditions are associated with congenital/developmental glaucoma?", a: "1. <b>Sturge-Weber syndrome</b> — port-wine stain (V1), ipsilateral glaucoma, leptomeningeal angioma<br>2. <b>Aniridia</b> — absent iris, associated with <b>Wilms tumor</b> (WAGR syndrome)<br>3. <b>Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome</b> — posterior embryotoxon, iris abnormalities, dental/facial anomalies<br>4. <b>Peters anomaly</b> — central corneal opacity, iridocorneal adhesions<br>5. <b>Neurofibromatosis type 1</b>" },
{ q: "What is Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome and its association with glaucoma?", a: "<b>Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome</b> — spectrum of anterior segment dysgenesis:<br>- <b>Posterior embryotoxon</b> (prominent Schwalbe line)<br>- <b>Iris strands</b> bridging to Schwalbe line<br>- <b>Iris abnormalities</b> (corectopia, polycoria, iris hypoplasia)<br>- <b>Systemic</b>: dental hypoplasia, maxillary hypoplasia, periumbilical skin redundancy<br>Glaucoma develops in <b>~50%</b>." },
{ q: "How does Sturge-Weber syndrome cause glaucoma?", a: "Mechanisms:<br>1. <b>Elevated episcleral venous pressure</b> (from episcleral hemangioma) → decreased aqueous outflow → <b>open-angle glaucoma</b> (most common in older children/adults)<br>2. <b>Angle anomaly</b> (trabeculodysgenesis) → <b>congenital-type glaucoma</b> (infants)<br>Occurs in <b>~30–70%</b> of Sturge-Weber patients with V1 involvement." },

// ===== GLAUCOMA SUSPECT & OHT =====
{ q: "What defines ocular hypertension (OHT)?", a: "<b>OHT</b> = <b>IOP >21 mmHg</b> on multiple occasions with:<br>- <b>Normal optic nerves</b> (no cupping)<br>- <b>Normal visual fields</b><br>- <b>Open angles</b> on gonioscopy<br>Not all OHT patients develop glaucoma — <b>~10% at 5 years</b> if untreated (OHTS study)." },
{ q: "What is a glaucoma suspect?", a: "A patient with one or more findings suggesting possible glaucoma but <b>no definitive diagnosis</b>:<br>- <b>Elevated IOP</b> (OHT)<br>- <b>Suspicious optic disc</b> (large cup, asymmetry)<br>- <b>Family history</b><br>- <b>Thin CCT</b><br>- <b>Borderline visual fields</b><br>Requires <b>serial monitoring</b> with VF, OCT, and IOP to detect progression." },
{ q: "According to the OHTS study, what factors increase the risk of OHT converting to POAG?", a: "<b>OHTS risk factors for conversion</b>:<br>1. <b>Higher baseline IOP</b><br>2. <b>Older age</b><br>3. <b>Larger C/D ratio</b><br>4. <b>Thinner CCT</b> (<555 μm)<br>5. <b>Higher PSD</b> on visual field<br>Treatment with topical IOP-lowering reduced conversion risk by <b>~50%</b> over 5 years." },

// ===== KEY CLINICAL TRIALS =====
{ q: "What did the Ocular Hypertension Treatment Study (OHTS) demonstrate?", a: "<b>OHTS</b>: topical IOP-lowering medication in OHT patients reduced the risk of developing POAG by <b>~50%</b> over 5 years (from 9.5% to 4.4%).<br>Identified <b>5 risk factors</b>: older age, higher IOP, thinner CCT, larger vertical C/D ratio, higher PSD." },
{ q: "What was the main finding of the Early Manifest Glaucoma Trial (EMGT)?", a: "<b>EMGT</b>: treatment (betaxolol + ALT) reduced risk of glaucoma <b>progression by ~50%</b> in early OAG.<br>Every <b>1 mmHg of IOP reduction</b> decreased progression risk by <b>~10%</b>.<br>Showed that <b>higher baseline IOP, exfoliation, bilateral disease, and disc hemorrhage</b> were risk factors for progression." },
{ q: "What did the Collaborative Normal-Tension Glaucoma Study (CNTGS) show?", a: "<b>CNTGS</b>: <b>IOP lowering is beneficial even in NTG</b>.<br>A <b>30% IOP reduction</b> from baseline significantly reduced the rate of visual field progression.<br>Confirmed IOP as a modifiable risk factor <b>regardless of whether baseline IOP is normal</b>." },
{ q: "What were the AGIS and CIGTS trials?", a: "<b>AGIS (Advanced Glaucoma Intervention Study)</b>: compared ALT → trabeculectomy vs trabeculectomy → ALT sequences. Found <b>lower IOP = better field preservation</b>. Eyes with IOP consistently <18 had <b>no VF progression</b>.<br><b>CIGTS (Collaborative Initial Glaucoma Treatment Study)</b>: compared initial medicine vs surgery. Both effective; surgery achieved <b>lower IOP</b> but more cataracts." },
{ q: "What was the LiGHT trial's contribution to glaucoma management?", a: "<b>LiGHT (Laser in Glaucoma and Ocular Hypertension)</b> trial: demonstrated that <b>initial SLT</b> was at least as effective as <b>initial eye drops</b> for newly diagnosed OAG/OHT over 3 years.<br>Supported SLT as a viable <b>first-line treatment</b> — better IOP control, fewer drops needed, cost-effective." },

// ===== ADDITIONAL HIGH-YIELD CARDS =====
{ q: "What is the difference between open-angle and angle-closure glaucoma on gonioscopy?", a: "<b>Open-angle</b>: TM and other angle structures are <b>visible</b> (Shaffer grade III–IV). Outflow resistance is within the TM itself.<br><b>Angle-closure</b>: peripheral iris <b>apposes/occludes the TM</b> (Shaffer grade 0–I). TM is not accessible for aqueous drainage." },
{ q: "What are peripheral anterior synechiae (PAS) and why are they important?", a: "<b>PAS</b> = adhesions of the <b>peripheral iris to the trabecular meshwork/cornea</b>.<br>Cause <b>permanent angle closure</b> even after the underlying cause is treated.<br>Seen in: chronic angle closure, NVG, inflammatory glaucoma, ICE syndrome.<br>Detected on <b>gonioscopy</b>." },
{ q: "What is the iridocorneal endothelial (ICE) syndrome?", a: "<b>ICE syndrome</b>: a group of unilateral conditions with <b>abnormal corneal endothelium</b> that proliferates across the angle and iris.<br>Subtypes: <b>Chandler syndrome, progressive iris atrophy, Cogan-Reese (iris nevus)</b><br>Causes secondary angle-closure glaucoma from PAS. Typically <b>young women</b>." },
{ q: "What is Posner-Schlossman syndrome (glaucomatocyclitic crisis)?", a: "<b>Posner-Schlossman</b> = recurrent episodes of <b>mild anterior uveitis</b> with <b>markedly elevated IOP</b> (40–60 mmHg).<br>Features: <b>unilateral, few fine KPs, minimal flare, open angle, mild symptoms</b> despite high IOP.<br>Self-limited, treated with <b>topical steroids + IOP-lowering drops</b>. Associated with <b>CMV infection</b>." },
{ q: "Which medications should be avoided in patients at risk for angle closure?", a: "Avoid drugs that cause <b>pupil dilation (mydriasis)</b>:<br>- <b>Anticholinergics</b>: atropine, scopolamine, ipratropium<br>- <b>Sympathomimetics</b>: phenylephrine, pseudoephedrine<br>- <b>Antihistamines, TCAs, SSRIs, topiramate</b><br>Key: these are <b>safe</b> in open-angle glaucoma. Only angle-closure is at risk." },
{ q: "How does topiramate cause acute angle-closure glaucoma?", a: "<b>Topiramate</b> causes <b>ciliary body swelling</b> and <b>forward rotation of the lens-iris diaphragm</b> → <b>non-pupillary block mechanism</b>.<br>Bilateral, occurs within <b>first 1–2 weeks</b> of starting the drug.<br>Treatment: <b>stop topiramate</b> + cycloplegics (to push lens back) + IOP-lowering agents. <b>LPI is NOT effective</b> (no pupillary block)." },
{ q: "What is malignant glaucoma (aqueous misdirection)?", a: "<b>Malignant glaucoma</b> = aqueous humor is misdirected <b>posteriorly into the vitreous</b> instead of anteriorly → ciliolenticular block → shallow/flat AC with high IOP.<br>Occurs after <b>intraocular surgery</b> (especially filtering surgery) in eyes with narrow angles.<br>Treatment: <b>cycloplegics (atropine) + aqueous suppressants ± vitrectomy</b>. <b>Miotics are contraindicated</b>." },
{ q: "What is the Seidel test?", a: "The <b>Seidel test</b> detects <b>wound leaks or bleb leaks</b>.<br>Apply <b>fluorescein strip</b> to the suspected leak site. Under <b>cobalt blue light</b>, aqueous humor streaming through will <b>dilute the fluorescein</b>, creating a <b>green rivulet</b> (positive Seidel test).<br>Important post-trabeculectomy and post-trauma." },
{ q: "What role do antimetabolites play in glaucoma surgery?", a: "<b>Mitomycin C (MMC)</b> and <b>5-fluorouracil (5-FU)</b> are antimetabolites applied during/after <b>trabeculectomy</b> to prevent <b>subconjunctival fibrosis</b> and bleb failure.<br><b>MMC</b>: more potent, single intraoperative application<br><b>5-FU</b>: can be given as subconjunctival injections postop<br>Risk: thin, avascular blebs → late-onset leaks and infection." },
{ q: "How is ghost cell glaucoma treated?", a: "<b>Ghost cell glaucoma</b>: caused by <b>denatured (khaki-colored) RBCs</b> from an old vitreous hemorrhage that migrate to the AC and clog the TM.<br>Occurs <b>weeks to months</b> after vitreous hemorrhage.<br>Treatment: <b>anterior chamber washout</b> if medical therapy fails; <b>vitrectomy</b> to remove the source." },
{ q: "What is the van Herick technique?", a: "<b>Van Herick technique</b> = slit-lamp method to <b>estimate the AC depth</b> (peripheral anterior chamber depth) by comparing the <b>ratio of AC depth to corneal thickness</b> at the limbus.<br>Grade 4: AC ≥ corneal thickness (wide open)<br>Grade 1: AC < ¼ corneal thickness → <b>dangerously narrow</b>, gonioscopy needed." },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of action of epinephrine/dipivefrin in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Dipivefrin</b> (prodrug of epinephrine) — <b>rarely used now</b>.<br>Mechanism: stimulates <b>α and β receptors</b> → <b>decreases aqueous production</b> (β2) + <b>increases trabecular & uveoscleral outflow</b><br>Avoid in: <b>angle-closure glaucoma</b> (mydriasis) and <b>aphakia</b> (CME risk)." },
{ q: "Which glaucoma medication is known to cause a cosmetic effect used commercially?", a: "<b>Bimatoprost (Lumigan)</b> — a prostaglandin analog.<br>Side effects of <b>eyelash growth</b> (longer, thicker, darker lashes) led to its cosmetic formulation: <b>Latisse</b> (bimatoprost 0.03%).<br>Other PGA-related cosmetic concern: <b>periorbital fat atrophy</b> (prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy)." },
{ q: "What is apraclonidine used for in ophthalmology?", a: "<b>Apraclonidine</b> (alpha-2 agonist, less selective than brimonidine):<br>Used for <b>short-term IOP control</b> and <b>prevention of post-laser IOP spikes</b> (given before SLT, LPI, etc.).<br>Not for long-term use due to high rate of <b>tachyphylaxis</b> and <b>allergic blepharoconjunctivitis</b>.<br>Also used in <b>Horner syndrome diagnosis</b> (reversal of miosis/ptosis in postganglionic Horner)." },
{ q: "What is the significance of disc hemorrhage in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Drance hemorrhage</b> (disc/splinter hemorrhage) = small flame-shaped hemorrhage at the <b>optic disc margin</b>.<br>Indicates <b>active glaucomatous damage</b> and predicts <b>future progression</b>.<br>More common in <b>NTG</b>. Often at the <b>inferotemporal or superotemporal</b> disc margin.<br>Requires <b>intensification of treatment</b>." },
{ q: "How is glaucoma classified into open-angle and closed-angle types?", a: "<b>Open-angle glaucoma</b>:<br>- POAG, NTG, steroid-induced, pseudoexfoliation, pigmentary, phacolytic, ghost cell<br><b>Angle-closure glaucoma</b>:<br>- Primary angle closure (acute/chronic), phacomorphic, NVG (late), iris bombé, ICE syndrome, plateau iris<br>Classification is based on <b>gonioscopy</b> findings." },
{ q: "What visual field defect respects the horizontal meridian?", a: "<b>Glaucomatous visual field defects</b> respect the <b>horizontal meridian</b> (nasal step, arcuate scotoma) because the <b>nerve fiber layer</b> is divided along the horizontal raphe in the temporal retina.<br>This distinguishes glaucoma from <b>neurologic causes</b>, which respect the <b>vertical meridian</b>." },
{ q: "What is the role of latanoprostene bunod in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Latanoprostene bunod (Vyzulta)</b> — a <b>prostaglandin analog that also releases nitric oxide (NO)</b>.<br>Dual mechanism: <b>increases uveoscleral outflow</b> (PGA component) + <b>increases trabecular outflow</b> (NO relaxes TM/Schlemm canal).<br>May provide greater IOP reduction than latanoprost alone." },
{ q: "What systemic medication can cause IOP elevation mimicking glaucoma?", a: "<b>Corticosteroids</b> (most common cause of drug-induced IOP elevation):<br>- Topical ophthalmic steroids (highest risk)<br>- Periocular injections<br>- Systemic (oral/IV) steroids<br>- Inhaled steroids (lower risk)<br><b>~30% of the population</b> are steroid responders. IOP usually <b>normalizes within weeks</b> of discontinuation." },
{ q: "What is Schwartz-Matsuo syndrome?", a: "<b>Schwartz-Matsuo syndrome</b> = elevated IOP + <b>rhegmatogenous retinal detachment</b>.<br>Mechanism: photoreceptor outer segments released from detached retina → migrate anteriorly → <b>clog the trabecular meshwork</b> → open-angle glaucoma.<br>IOP normalizes after <b>retinal detachment repair</b>." },
{ q: "What medications can be used to lower IOP in hyphema, and which should be avoided in sickle cell patients?", a: "<b>For hyphema</b>: timolol, brimonidine, topical steroids, cycloplegics<br><b>Avoid in sickle cell patients</b>:<br>- <b>CAIs (acetazolamide)</b> — worsens sickling (acidosis, concentrated aqueous)<br>- <b>Epinephrine/dipivefrin</b> — may worsen sickling<br>- <b>Miotics</b> — may worsen inflammation<br>Sickled RBCs clog the TM and may cause <b>optic nerve damage at lower IOPs</b>." },
{ q: "What is the role of ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) in glaucoma?", a: "<b>UBM</b> uses <b>high-frequency ultrasound (50 MHz)</b> to image <b>anterior segment structures</b> including the angle, ciliary body, and lens position.<br>Useful for: <b>plateau iris, ciliary body tumors, cyclodialysis cleft, phacomorphic glaucoma, angle assessment</b> when gonioscopy is limited (e.g., corneal edema)." },
{ q: "What are the stages of primary angle closure disease?", a: "Spectrum from narrow to closed:<br>1. <b>Primary angle closure suspect (PACS)</b>: narrow angle, ≥2 quadrants of iridotrabecular contact, no PAS, normal IOP<br>2. <b>Primary angle closure (PAC)</b>: ≥2 quadrants iridotrabecular contact + ↑IOP or PAS, but <b>no optic nerve damage</b><br>3. <b>PACG</b>: PAC + <b>glaucomatous optic neuropathy</b> and/or VF loss" },
{ q: "What is a cyclodialysis cleft?", a: "<b>Cyclodialysis cleft</b> = separation of the <b>ciliary body from the scleral spur</b>, usually after <b>trauma</b>.<br>Creates a direct communication between AC and <b>suprachoroidal space</b> → <b>hypotony</b> (low IOP).<br>However, when the cleft <b>closes spontaneously</b>, IOP can <b>spike dramatically</b>." },
{ q: "How does the ISNT rule help differentiate glaucoma from physiologic cupping?", a: "<b>Physiologic cupping</b>: large cup but rim follows <b>ISNT rule</b> (I>S>N>T), symmetrical, no notching<br><b>Glaucomatous cupping</b>: <b>violation of ISNT rule</b>, especially inferior or inferotemporal rim thinning/notching, asymmetric C/D between eyes, disc hemorrhage, RNFL defects" },
{ q: "What is the significance of the Sampaolesi line on gonioscopy?", a: "The <b>Sampaolesi line</b> = <b>pigment anterior to Schwalbe line</b> on gonioscopy.<br>It is a hallmark of <b>pseudoexfoliation syndrome</b>.<br>(In pigmentary glaucoma, heavy homogeneous pigmentation is seen <b>at the TM level</b>, not anterior to Schwalbe line.)" },
{ q: "What is the typical visual field progression pattern in end-stage glaucoma?", a: "End-stage glaucoma has:<br>1. <b>Severe generalized constriction</b> ('tunnel vision')<br>2. Only a <b>central island</b> and/or <b>temporal island</b> of vision remaining<br>3. Eventually total <b>visual field loss</b> and <b>blindness</b><br>Central acuity may be preserved even with only <b>5–10° of central field</b> remaining." },
{ q: "What is the 'wipe-out' phenomenon in glaucoma surgery?", a: "<b>Wipe-out</b> = sudden, unexplained loss of <b>central vision</b> after glaucoma surgery (especially trabeculectomy) in patients with <b>advanced glaucoma</b> and a small central island of vision.<br>Etiology unclear — possibly related to <b>hypotony</b>, <b>choroidal effusion</b>, or <b>optic nerve ischemia</b>. Rare but devastating." },
{ q: "What is the pathophysiology of POAG at the cellular level?", a: "In POAG, increased <b>outflow resistance in the juxtacanalicular tissue</b> of the trabecular meshwork leads to elevated IOP.<br>Elevated IOP → <b>mechanical compression of axons at the lamina cribrosa</b> → <b>axoplasmic flow disruption</b> → reduced neurotrophic factor delivery → <b>retinal ganglion cell apoptosis</b>." },
{ q: "What is the relationship between myopia and glaucoma?", a: "<b>Myopia</b> is an independent risk factor for POAG because:<br>1. <b>Larger, thinner lamina cribrosa</b> → more susceptible to IOP-induced damage<br>2. <b>Tilted disc</b> may mimic or mask glaucomatous changes<br>3. <b>Larger optic disc</b> — harder to detect early cupping<br>4. <b>Thinner RNFL</b> at baseline" },
{ q: "What is the significance of an afferent pupillary defect (APD) in glaucoma?", a: "A <b>relative APD (Marcus Gunn pupil)</b> indicates <b>asymmetric optic nerve damage</b> between the two eyes.<br>In glaucoma, an APD suggests <b>significant unilateral or asymmetric</b> optic nerve damage.<br>Tested with the <b>swinging flashlight test</b>. A new or worsening APD warrants <b>closer monitoring and possible treatment intensification</b>." },
{ q: "Why should timolol be used with caution in neonates?", a: "<b>Timolol</b> (non-selective β-blocker) can cause <b>systemic β-blockade</b> in neonates even with topical application due to:<br>1. <b>Low body weight</b> (higher relative dose)<br>2. <b>Immature drug metabolism</b><br>Risks: <b>apnea, bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm, hypoglycemia</b>.<br>Use <b>low concentrations</b> (0.25%) and <b>punctal occlusion</b> to minimize absorption." },
{ q: "What is the difference between goniotomy and trabeculotomy?", a: "<b>Goniotomy</b>: <b>ab interno</b> approach — incision into TM under <b>direct gonioscopic visualization</b> through a clear cornea<br><b>Trabeculotomy</b>: <b>ab externo</b> approach — cannulate <b>Schlemm canal</b> from outside, then rotate probe to break through TM<br>Both are used for <b>congenital glaucoma</b>. Trabeculotomy preferred when <b>cornea is cloudy</b>." },
{ q: "What are the differences between Ahmed valve and Baerveldt implant?", a: "<b>Ahmed valve</b>:<br>- <b>Valved</b> (flow-restricted) → less early hypotony<br>- Can function <b>immediately</b><br>- Smaller plate (184 mm²)<br><br><b>Baerveldt implant</b>:<br>- <b>Non-valved</b> → needs suture ligation initially<br>- Larger plate (250–350 mm²) → potentially <b>lower long-term IOP</b><br>- Higher rate of early complications but better long-term IOP control (ABC/AVB studies)" },
{ q: "What medications should be avoided in a patient with sulfa allergy and glaucoma?", a: "<b>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</b> should be avoided:<br>- Topical: <b>dorzolamide, brinzolamide</b><br>- Oral: <b>acetazolamide, methazolamide</b><br>These are <b>sulfonamide derivatives</b>. Cross-reactivity risk exists, though it may be low. Use alternative agents (PGAs, beta-blockers, alpha agonists)." },
{ q: "What is the OHTS calculator used for?", a: "The <b>OHTS calculator</b> estimates a patient's <b>5-year risk of developing POAG</b> from OHT.<br>Inputs: <b>age, IOP, CCT, vertical C/D ratio, PSD</b>.<br>Helps guide treatment decisions: patients with <b>>20% 5-year risk</b> benefit from treatment; <b><10%</b> may be observed." },
{ q: "What is the pathophysiology behind steroid responders?", a: "<b>Steroid responders</b> (~30% of the general population, higher in POAG patients):<br>Corticosteroids induce <b>increased glycosaminoglycan deposition</b> and <b>myocilin accumulation</b> in the TM → decreased phagocytic activity → <b>increased outflow resistance</b> → elevated IOP.<br>Response is <b>dose- and duration-dependent</b>. Usually reversible upon stopping the steroid." },
{ q: "What is the role of neuroprotection in glaucoma research?", a: "<b>Neuroprotection</b> aims to protect <b>retinal ganglion cells</b> from apoptosis independent of IOP lowering.<br>Agents studied: <b>brimonidine</b> (Low-Pressure Glaucoma Treatment Study suggested possible neuroprotective effect), <b>memantine</b> (failed in clinical trials), <b>BDNF, citicoline</b>.<br>Currently, <b>IOP lowering remains the only proven strategy</b>." },
{ q: "A young myopic male presents with blurred vision and halos after exercise, elevated IOP, and a Krukenberg spindle. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Pigmentary glaucoma</b><br>Key features: <b>young, myopic, male</b> + post-exercise symptoms (pigment release) + <b>Krukenberg spindle</b> (vertical endothelial pigment) + <b>concave iris</b> + mid-peripheral iris transillumination defects + heavy TM pigmentation.<br>Exercise liberates pigment from the posterior iris, acutely clogging the TM." },
{ q: "A patient on long-term topical dexamethasone for uveitis develops elevated IOP with open angles and no synechiae. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Steroid-induced glaucoma</b><br>Open angle with no structural angle abnormality. IOP elevation is due to <b>increased TM resistance</b> from steroid-induced changes.<br>Management: <b>switch to a lower-potency steroid</b> (fluorometholone, loteprednol), add <b>IOP-lowering drops</b>, consider stopping steroids if possible. IOP usually <b>normalizes within weeks</b>." },
{ q: "An elderly patient presents with a white, flaky material on the pupillary margin and anterior lens capsule, elevated IOP, and poor mydriasis. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Pseudoexfoliation (PXF) syndrome with secondary glaucoma</b><br>Hallmark: <b>white, dandruff-like fibrillar material</b> on lens, pupillary margin, and TM.<br>Associated with <b>zonular weakness</b> (risk of lens subluxation during cataract surgery), <b>poor dilation</b>, and <b>higher IOP levels</b>.<br>Sampaolesi line on gonioscopy." },
{ q: "A 2-month-old infant presents with tearing, photophobia, and a large, cloudy cornea. What is the most likely diagnosis?", a: "<b>Primary congenital glaucoma</b><br>Classic triad: <b>epiphora, photophobia, blepharospasm</b> from corneal edema.<br>Signs: <b>buphthalmos</b> (large eye), <b>Haab striae</b> (Descemet breaks), <b>corneal edema/clouding</b>, elevated IOP.<br>Treatment: <b>surgical</b> — goniotomy or trabeculotomy." },
{ q: "A patient with a history of ischemic CRVO 3 months ago presents with a red, painful eye and IOP of 55 mmHg. Exam shows iris neovascularization. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Neovascular glaucoma (100-day glaucoma)</b><br>Ischemic CRVO → retinal ischemia → <b>VEGF release</b> → <b>rubeosis iridis</b> → fibrovascular membrane over TM → angle closure.<br>Treatment: <b>anti-VEGF injection + PRP + IOP-lowering agents ± tube shunt surgery</b>." },
{ q: "A patient presents with acute eye pain, nausea, halos, and a fixed mid-dilated pupil after watching a movie in a dark theater. IOP is 60 mmHg. Diagnosis and management?", a: "Diagnosis: <b>Acute primary angle-closure glaucoma</b><br>Precipitant: dim illumination → mid-dilation → pupillary block<br>Management:<br>1. <b>Timolol + brimonidine + pilocarpine 2%</b> (after IOP starts dropping)<br>2. <b>Topical steroid</b><br>3. <b>Oral/IV acetazolamide</b><br>4. <b>IV mannitol if refractory</b><br>5. Definitive: <b>LPI to BOTH eyes</b>" },
{ q: "What is the difference between a filtering bleb and an encapsulated bleb?", a: "<b>Filtering (functioning) bleb</b>: <b>diffuse, thin-walled, cystic</b> elevation of conjunctiva over the trabeculectomy site → good IOP control<br><b>Encapsulated (Tenon's cyst) bleb</b>: <b>dome-shaped, thick-walled</b>, forms 2–8 weeks postop, high IOP due to limited filtration → may respond to <b>aqueous suppressants, massage, or needling</b>" },
{ q: "What does the Glaucoma Hemifield Test (GHT) assess?", a: "The <b>GHT</b> is part of the Humphrey visual field printout. It compares corresponding regions in the <b>superior and inferior hemifields</b>.<br>Results: <b>'Outside normal limits'</b> = significant asymmetry → highly suggestive of glaucoma<br><b>'Borderline'</b>, <b>'Within normal limits'</b>, or <b>'Abnormally high sensitivity'</b><br>Useful for <b>early detection</b> of glaucomatous VF loss." },
{ q: "What are the reliability indices on a Humphrey visual field and what do they mean?", a: "<b>Fixation losses</b>: >20% = unreliable (patient not fixating)<br><b>False positives</b>: >15% = trigger-happy patient (responses without stimulus)<br><b>False negatives</b>: >33% = unreliable (no response to suprathreshold stimulus in previously seen area — can also indicate advanced disease)<br>Always check reliability before interpreting the VF." },
{ q: "What is peripapillary atrophy and its significance in glaucoma?", a: "<b>Peripapillary atrophy (PPA)</b>:<br><b>Zone beta</b> (inner): visible sclera and large choroidal vessels, associated with <b>glaucomatous damage</b> — enlarges with progression<br><b>Zone alpha</b> (outer): irregular pigmentation, less specific<br>Progressive zone beta PPA correlates with <b>RNFL loss</b> and is a risk factor for <b>glaucoma progression</b>." },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of mannitol in acute angle-closure treatment?", a: "<b>Mannitol</b> = <b>osmotic agent</b> given IV (1–2 g/kg).<br>Creates an <b>osmotic gradient</b> → draws water from the vitreous humor into the bloodstream → <b>reduces vitreous volume</b> → <b>lowers IOP</b>.<br>Used in <b>emergencies</b> when topical/oral agents are insufficient.<br>Contraindicated in <b>renal failure and CHF</b>." },
{ q: "Why is gonioscopy performed on BOTH eyes in glaucoma evaluation?", a: "1. In <b>angle closure</b>: fellow eye has <b>similar anatomy</b> and high risk of attack → prophylactic LPI<br>2. In <b>angle recession</b>: comparison needed — the <b>fellow eye serves as the normal reference</b> for angle width<br>3. Baseline documentation for <b>progression monitoring</b><br>4. Detect <b>asymmetric disease</b> (e.g., unilateral PXF, NVG)." },
];
